1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,402
Make the attack a little faster. It is a
familiar request during development.

2
00:00:04,402 --> 00:00:07,992
Should we shorten the preparation, or the
pause after impact?

3
00:00:07,992 --> 00:00:11,582
Consider a jump in Mega Man 11 or an attack in
Monster Hunter Rise.

4
00:00:11,582 --> 00:00:14,342
One action contains a beginning, a change, and
an ending.

5
00:00:14,342 --> 00:00:19,222
We will translate that feeling into a time
scale measured in frames.

6
00:00:19,222 --> 00:00:22,482
The games illustrate observable motion.

7
00:00:22,482 --> 00:00:25,192
We will verify exact numbers in an original
frame-timing test.

8
00:00:25,192 --> 00:00:28,960
The number of drawings is not necessarily the
game’s time scale.

9
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:32,053
Every frame count needs a reference rate.

10
00:00:32,053 --> 00:00:36,782
At sixty steps per second, twelve frames
represent zero point two seconds.

11
00:00:36,782 --> 00:00:40,682
At thirty steps per second, the same twelve
frames represent zero point four seconds.

12
00:00:40,682 --> 00:00:43,942
The count stays the same, but the elapsed time
doubles.

13
00:00:43,942 --> 00:00:48,232
Our test therefore uses a fixed
sixty-update-per-second rule.

14
00:00:48,232 --> 00:00:52,272
It displays elapsed time alongside a moving
marker.

15
00:00:52,272 --> 00:00:56,272
Record the frame count and its reference rate
together in your design notes.

16
00:00:56,272 --> 00:00:59,920
Rendering and game-rule updates can run at
different rates.

17
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:03,442
Now decide which part of an attack you are
measuring.

18
00:01:03,442 --> 00:01:07,652
Our original test records input acceptance as
time zero.

19
00:01:07,652 --> 00:01:10,523
The hit area begins after twelve time steps,

20
00:01:10,523 --> 00:01:14,282
stays active for three steps, then recovers
for fifteen.

21
00:01:14,282 --> 00:01:16,092
The total is thirty steps, or zero point five
seconds.

22
00:01:16,092 --> 00:01:19,582
If the request for speed concerns preparation,

23
00:01:19,582 --> 00:01:23,482
we can shorten that phase and keep the others
unchanged.

24
00:01:23,482 --> 00:01:27,542
Record both boundaries so everyone measures
the same interval.

25
00:01:27,542 --> 00:01:31,920
These prototype numbers do not represent move
data from another game.

26
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:35,552
Does adding drawings make the hit happen
sooner?

27
00:01:35,552 --> 00:01:39,472
We changed only the poses in the same attack.

28
00:01:39,472 --> 00:01:42,082
One version holds fewer poses longer; the
other changes poses more often.

29
00:01:42,082 --> 00:01:46,502
Both hit areas begin at the same twelfth time
step.

30
00:01:46,502 --> 00:01:51,312
Their visual smoothness differs, but the wait
for the hit is equal.

31
00:01:51,312 --> 00:01:55,322
We distinguish the animation-pose clock from
the hit-area clock.

32
00:01:55,322 --> 00:01:59,082
Before adding poses, decide which timing you
want to change.

33
00:01:59,082 --> 00:02:03,280
The implementation and logs verify that poses
and collision timing are separate.

34
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,682
Next, examine a brief pause at impact.

35
00:02:07,682 --> 00:02:12,212
In our test, only the combat clock pauses for
four steps after collision.

36
00:02:12,212 --> 00:02:14,822
The background time indicator keeps moving.

37
00:02:14,822 --> 00:02:16,122
The attack data still spans thirty combat
steps,

38
00:02:16,122 --> 00:02:20,362
but the actual completion time also includes
the pause.

39
00:02:20,362 --> 00:02:25,382
When observing a paused action, ask which
clock the count refers to.

40
00:02:25,382 --> 00:02:28,582
This does not imply that every commercial game
uses this method.

41
00:02:28,582 --> 00:02:33,280
It illustrates explicitly choosing what pauses
and what continues in our own game.

42
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,192
Finally, frames counted in a recording are
only one kind of evidence.

43
00:02:38,192 --> 00:02:42,702
Repeated or missing pictures can obscure the
game’s internal timing.

44
00:02:42,702 --> 00:02:45,772
Even at different display update rates,

45
00:02:45,772 --> 00:02:49,422
the same fixed hit-area clock produces the hit
at the same elapsed time.

46
00:02:49,422 --> 00:02:52,442
First, watch at normal speed and estimate the
duration.

47
00:02:52,442 --> 00:02:56,282
Then record input acceptance, the first active
hit, and when another action is allowed.

48
00:02:56,282 --> 00:02:59,462
Check the reference rate alongside the
measured elapsed time

49
00:02:59,462 --> 00:03:02,752
to turn a vague request for speed into a
change everyone can review.

50
00:03:02,752 --> 00:03:05,600
Find it by feel, then verify it by
measurement.
